<pdd-reason>The FastAPI application itself: builds the clients, resolves who the caller is, and guarantees the error envelope. The only module that turns configuration into a running service.</pdd-reason>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"functions":[
  {"name":"app","signature":"app: FastAPI","returns":"the ASGI application, importable as backend.main:app"},
  {"name":"lifespan","signature":"@asynccontextmanager async def lifespan(app: FastAPI)","returns":"builds Supabase + Redis, binds them to the deps providers, closes both on shutdown"},
  {"name":"resolve_caller","signature":"async def resolve_caller(request: Request, call_next) -> Response","returns":"HTTP middleware; stamps request.state.user_id with the caller id or None"}
]}}
</pdd-interface>

<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/config_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/backend/deps_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/backend/routes/features_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/backend/routes/votes_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

Implement `backend/main.py`. Assembly only — every behaviour lives in a route module.

<responsibility>
Construct the clients once, resolve caller identity once, register the routers, and make every
error response the frozen envelope.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT implement an endpoint, query Postgres, or touch Redis beyond constructing
the client — routes own all three.
This module DOES NOT declare `Depends` providers; `backend/deps.py` declares them and this module
only binds real clients into them.
This module DOES NOT read `os.environ`; `shared/config.py` has already read, typed, and frozen
every setting.
This module DOES NOT call an LLM or reach the orchestrator, ever.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
**Binding** — installing a real client into `app.dependency_overrides` for `get_supabase` /
`get_redis`. Until it happens those providers raise `RuntimeError` by design (deps R1).
**Caller id** — the UUID string identifying who is making the request, resolved here and nowhere
else. `None` for an anonymous reader, which is the normal case on the public board.
**Identified, not registered** — a caller id is proof of a session, not of an account. There is no
signup, profile, or accounts table in the MVP (US-01).
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY construct the Supabase and Redis clients, once, in the lifespan.
- MAY read `request.headers` and verify a JWT — this is the one module allowed to.
- MUST NOT run a query, publish to Redis, or read a Redis key.
- MUST NOT define business rules that belong to a route.
</capabilities>

## Startup and shutdown

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Build the Supabase and Redis clients exactly once inside an `asynccontextmanager`
lifespan, and bind them by assigning into `app.dependency_overrides[get_supabase]` and
`[get_redis]`. A module-level client global MUST NOT exist — a test that imports this module
must not open a socket.
R2 (MUST): Close both clients on shutdown, each guarded so a failure closing one still closes the
other.
R3 (MUST): Take every value from the imported `settings` object. Re-reading the environment or
constructing a second `Settings` here is a defect even when the values would match.
R4 (MUST): Log one WARNING at startup when `DEV_MODE` is true, naming it as spoofable and
never-for-production. It is the switch that disables signature verification, so its state must be
visible in the logs of any environment it is on in.
</contract_rules>

## Caller identity (US-01: no account setup ceremony)

One HTTP middleware resolves the caller and stamps `request.state.user_id`. `backend/deps.py`
only reads that attribute, so this is the single place identity is decided.

<contract_rules>
R5 (MUST): Stamp `request.state.user_id` on **every** request — the resolved id, or `None`. It
MUST always be set, so `get_optional_user_id` never raises `AttributeError` on an anonymous read.
R6 (MUST NOT): Return `401` from the middleware, ever. A request with no usable credential
continues with `user_id = None`; whether that is fatal is the route's decision, made by depending
on `get_current_user_id` (401) or `get_optional_user_id` (None). Rejecting here would break the
anonymous board reads (US-05) that share the same middleware.
R7 (MUST): When `DEV_MODE` is false, verify the Supabase JWT signature and expiry using
`SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET` before trusting any claim, and take the id from the `sub` claim. A
malformed, expired, or unverifiable token resolves to `None` — never to a partially trusted id.
R8 (MUST): Treat a Supabase **anonymous** session's JWT as fully valid. It verifies identically to
a registered user's, and the middleware MUST NOT branch on an `is_anonymous` claim: pitching needs
a resolvable caller id, not a completed signup. This is the mechanism behind US-01's "no account
setup ceremony".
R9 (MUST): When `DEV_MODE` is true, skip verification entirely and take the id from the
`X-Dev-User` header, falling back to one fixed anonymous-session UUID when the header is absent.
This is what lets `curl` and the demo act as distinct users with no account management. It is
spoofable by design and MUST NOT be reachable when `DEV_MODE` is false.
R10 (MUST NOT): Log, echo, or place a caller id or a raw token in any response body, header, or
log line — including at DEBUG.
</contract_rules>

## Error envelope

Both route modules state that every error is `{"error": {"code", "message"}}` and never FastAPI's
default `{"detail"}`. A route can only honour that for errors it raises itself; validation
failures, unmatched paths, and unhandled exceptions are produced by the framework. Closing that
gap is this module's job.

<contract_rules>
R11 (MUST): Install exception handlers so `RequestValidationError`, `HTTPException`, and any
unhandled `Exception` all serialise to the envelope from `backend/deps.py`. A `{"detail": ...}`
body MUST NOT be reachable on any path.
R12 (MUST): Map a body that fails schema validation to `400` with `error.code`
`validation_failed` — not FastAPI's default `422`, which `openapi.yaml` reserves for
`not_votable` on the upvote route. Two different meanings on one status is a bug the frontend
cannot untangle.
R13 (MUST): Give the unhandled-`Exception` handler a `500` with fixed, friendly copy and no
exception text, stack frame, query, or pitch content. Log the detail server-side instead.
</contract_rules>

## Wiring

<contract_rules>
R14 (MUST): Register the `features` and `votes` routers, and only those two. Both declare the
same `/api/features` prefix; that is intended and they compose.
R15 (MUST): Restrict CORS to `FORUM_ORIGIN` only. A wildcard origin MUST NOT be used — writes
carry a bearer token.
R16 (MUST): Expose the app as the module-level name `app`, so the service starts as
`uvicorn backend.main:app` with no factory argument.
</contract_rules>

<pdd>
Scope note: this wires the two route modules that exist. The webhook (`/webhooks/render`, US-10),
the broadcast relay (US-11), and the sprint trigger (US-07) have no prompt in the current tree —
their routers are registered when they are written, and R14 is the line to update. Nothing else
in this module changes when they land, which is the point of keeping assembly this thin.

`/api/features/mine` lives inside `routes/features.py` rather than its own module specifically so
its declaration order against `/api/features/{feature_id}` is a local, visible property of that
file instead of a registration-order rule enforced here.
</pdd>

